From: Orion <orionopenwow@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] openvanilla: Herald's moves count as presence; his rides stay above hills

Herald (the realm's bot AI playing your character, ".bot self") moves the player by server
splines while the player watches with hands off the keys. Players' reports, 2026-10-21:

- "If I activate Herald, he puts my account into AFK mode." The idle timer counted only raw
  keyboard and mouse input, so after 5 minutes it sat the character down and marked it AFK, and
  after 30 it asked for a logout. A server ride now stamps the clock like input does
  (idle::ride_is_presence), except a taxi flight, which keeps the 1.12 timer.
- "He goes through hills." A long server path is drawn straight between navmesh points, and over
  a hill the line runs inside it, below the ride's 2.5 yd ground probe. When that probe finds no
  ground at all, a second one from 12 yd up puts the body on the hill. Ground just below the body
  still wins, so nobody is lifted onto a bridge or roof.

diff --git a/crates/benilla-app/src/player/server_ride.rs b/crates/benilla-app/src/player/server_ride.rs
index 78394ebb..787dabaf 100644
--- a/crates/benilla-app/src/player/server_ride.rs
+++ b/crates/benilla-app/src/player/server_ride.rs
@@ -14,13 +14,24 @@ use super::Player;
 
 /// The walkable surface under a ride's pose: a one-sided down-ray from the server's own Z, through
 /// the same window as `net::motion::spline`'s clamp.
+///
+/// Open Vanilla: when that window finds nothing, the server's line is buried in the ground: a long
+/// server path (Herald, the realm's bot AI playing this character, walks by them) draws straight
+/// between its navmesh points, and over a hill the line runs inside it, so the player was seen
+/// walking through hills (a player's report, 2026-10-21). A second down-ray from higher up puts
+/// the body on the hill's surface. It only runs when the first finds no ground at all, so a body
+/// under a bridge or a roof, with ground just below it, is never lifted onto them.
 fn ride_ground(world: &benilla_world::collision::WorldCollision, pos: Vec3) -> Option<f32> {
     const UP: f32 = 2.5;
     const DOWN: f32 = 4.0;
-    let origin = Vec3::new(pos.x, pos.y + UP, pos.z);
-    world
-        .ray_body(origin, Dir3::NEG_Y, UP + DOWN)
-        .map(|h| origin.y - h.distance)
+    const BURIED_UP: f32 = 12.0;
+    let probe = |up: f32| {
+        let origin = Vec3::new(pos.x, pos.y + up, pos.z);
+        world
+            .ray_body(origin, Dir3::NEG_Y, up + DOWN)
+            .map(|h| origin.y - h.distance)
+    };
+    probe(UP).or_else(|| probe(BURIED_UP))
 }
 
 /// The Z the ride stands at: the ground under the spline's XZ, not its vertical. The reference
